n-practice overflow, and `mb->data[i] = init_value + i` reproduces C's `int`→`size_t`→`int` round-trip.\n- Error-check order and the early `free_block`/`return -1` path are preserved verbatim.\n\n## One unmatchable case (documented, not a defect)\n`create_block` returns a 40-byte struct via sret. GCC builds the local on **its own stack and memcpy's all 40 bytes**, so bytes after `name`'s NUL (and the 3 padding bytes) are *indeterminate* — in the C they are residue from the previous `create_block` invocation's frame. No reimplementation can reproduce that (it would require matching GCC's `-O0` frame layout), and reading it is UB in C. I zero those bytes, which is deterministic and matches every *defined* observable (`id`, `flags`, `name` as a C string). This only shows up if a caller hexdumps past the NUL after having passed a 31-char name; all short-name cases matched the C exactly, since the region is zero there.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified (md5sums shown above), `cargo build --release` is clean with no warnings, and `Cargo.toml` sets `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` with `name = \"c_src\"` so the output is `libc_src.so`, matching the C library's name."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":963,"cache_read_input_tokens":42326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":963,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"369447f4-16c9-40ef-b6fc-4493b6f26995","uuid":"9578ed46-f46f-4293-9e51-470c942a1a08","timestamp":"2026-08-19T16:05:04.440Z"}
